- SET (set-based visibility) is claimed "strictly stronger" than the single-q tail predicate. Is that actually true as stated? Claim: gamma outside the union ⟹ no single q sees a tail; but not conversely. Strictly stronger = set of visible geodesics under SET ⊂ under tail? Wait. Let's parse: Visibility predicate defines which geodesics count. A class conclusion "no incomplete geodesic visible from I+" — with a *weaker* visibility predicate (fewer geodesics visible), the conclusion is *weaker* (easier to satisfy). SET says visible iff entirely contained in union of J^-(q). Tail predicate says visible iff some tail contained in a single J^-(q). If a tail is contained in J^-(q) for a single q then the whole geodesic is contained in the union (since the earlier part... wait, gamma is future-inextendible; any point of gamma is in J^-(q) for some q? Not necessarily for the prefix). Actually the claim "strictly stronger" means SET-visibility is a strictly stronger *condition on the geodesic* (fewer visible geodesics), so WCC with SET is a weaker conclusion than WCC with tail. Hmm, the taxonomy says "Strictly stronger than the parent class: gamma outside the union implies no single q sees a tail of gamma, but not conversely." Careful: "the two readings must never be interchanged."
